[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Atomic Variables, CAS Operations, and Lock-free Data Structures in Java"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Concurrency in Java has evolved far beyond traditional synchronized blocks and explicit locks. At the heart of modern high-performance concurrent programming lie atomic variables and non-blocking algorithms powered by Compare-And-Swap (CAS) instructions. This tutorial explores how to leverage java.util.concurrent.atomic to build lock-free data structures that bypass the performance penalties of thread contention and context switching."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. Understanding CAS (Compare-And-Swap)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"CAS is an atomic instruction executed at the hardware level by modern CPUs. It takes three operands: a memory location (V), the expected old value (A), and a new value (B). The processor atomically updates V to B only if the current value of V matches A. If it does not match, the operation does nothing. In either case, it returns the value currently held at V."},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"This mechanism enables optimistic concurrency. Instead of locking a resource and forcing other threads to wait (pessimistic locking), a thread attempts an update and, if it fails due to concurrent modification by another thread, retries the operation in a loop."},{"order":6,"type":"heading","text":"2. Atomic Variables in Java"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The java.util.concurrent.atomic package provides classes like AtomicInteger, AtomicLong, and AtomicReference that wrap primitive values and object references with low-level CAS capabilities. Here is a simple demonstration of using an AtomicInteger to implement a thread-safe counter without explicit locks."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class Counter {
    private final AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger(0);

    public void increment() {
        int current;
        do {
            current = count.get();
        } while (!count.compareAndSet(current, current + 1));
    }

    public int get() {
        return count.get();
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"heading","text":"3. Building a Lock-Free Stack"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"We can extend the CAS pattern beyond single variables to build complex data structures, such as a lock-free Treiber stack. By utilizing AtomicReference for the head pointer of the stack, multiple threads can push and pop nodes simultaneously without blocking."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicReference;

public class LockFreeStack<T> {
    private static class Node<T> {
        final T item;
        Node<T> next;

        Node(T item) {
            this.item = item;
        }
    }

    private final AtomicReference<Node<T>> head = new AtomicReference<>(null);

    public void push(T item) {
        Node<T> newHead = new Node<>(item);
        Node<T> oldHead;
        do {
            oldHead = head.get();
            newHead.next = oldHead;
        } while (!head.compareAndSet(oldHead, newHead));
    }

    public T pop() {
        Node<T> oldHead;
        Node<T> newHead;
        do {
            oldHead = head.get();
            if (oldHead == null) {
                return null;
            }
            newHead = oldHead.next;
        } while (!head.compareAndSet(oldHead, newHead));
        return oldHead.item;
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"heading","text":"4. The ABA Problem and Solutions"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"While CAS is powerful, it introduces a subtle vulnerability known as the ABA problem. If a variable reads value A, another thread changes it to B and then back to A, a CAS operation will succeed believing nothing changed. In data structures involving node reuse, this can lead to memory corruption or infinite loops."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"AtomicStampedReference: Associates an object reference with an integer stamp, updating both atomically to prevent ABA."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"AtomicMarkableReference: Associates a reference with a boolean mark, commonly used for lock-free linked lists to flag nodes slated for deletion."},{"order":16,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Atomic variables and CAS operations are foundational tools for writing scalable, non-blocking Java applications. By avoiding the overhead of thread blocking and context switches, lock-free data structures maximize throughput under high contention. However, they require careful design to handle retry loops and avoid pitfalls like the ABA problem."}]
